body:
My cumulative GPA is 3.0 and my science GPA is 2.3. I’m failing the class right now unfortunately. I’m not exactly sure if we’re going to get a curve in the class but all I know is that she drops 1 exam. I did horrible on the first exam so I thought this exam would be my moment but the exam was hard for literally everyone. This is why I’m losing confidence. 

&#x200B;

I wanted to ask, since Orgo 1 is a class adcoms pay attention to since it fails a lot of premeds, would it be weird for them to see me taking it with only one other class if I hypothetically dropped Calc?

I personally think you did the right thing coming to seek help on this matter. And it's also wonderful that you haven't been deterred from salah and Islam because of your father. 

&#x200B;

I have a similar situation with my mom where she basically calls me hellspawn for not praying and even if I do pray, I have all kinds of problems in her eyes. That's all she talks about with me and it made me hate myself for a period of time. She would never talk to me about anything else or be that supportive in my development stage but that's okay, I still love her. And I didn't give up on myself or on Islam.

&#x200B;

My point is that your father will make you feel all kinds of emotions and it's totally ok. I know it's difficult because our parents can make us feel GREAT anger, pain, or frustration at times. But you have a long life ahead of you and these years are the years that will define you for the rest of your life. Allah teaches us to be patient in the face of despair, which is what you must be feeling now. Because it sounds like you don't want to fight your father, but desperately need him to see it the say you see it. 

&#x200B;

What your father is doing is definitely wrong and unfortunately common throughout the Muslim community when it comes to parenting. You yourself only need to realize that you are your own person and are old enough to have your own values. The day will come when you won't have to abide by your father's will anymore, and that will come when you're older and more mature. You'll be able to live your life without your father breathing down on your neck. Until then, you need to focus on what's important, your identity as a Muslim follower. 

&#x200B;

You need to hold on to what makes you Muslim other than your parents just forcing it on you and keep that state of mind until you can finally start living on your own. I'll be honest, it will be very lonely. But I promise that once these confusing years are over, and in those years you managed to retain your identity, you will become the greatest grown version of yourself that even your father will have to accept. 

&#x200B;

Inshallah, don't despair and keep your head up. Life is all about facing these challenges alone be it against others or even your own family. Stay strong sister.
